A Go map can use `*ListNode` as its key, so it stores the actual node pointer rather than the node value. That matters because two different nodes may contain the same value.

The walk stops at `nil` for a normal list. Otherwise, a pointer already present in `seen` proves that the traversal has entered a loop.

```go
/*
Definition for singly-linked list.
type ListNode struct {
    Val  int
    Next *ListNode
}
*/

func HasCycle(head *ListNode) bool {
    seen := make(map[*ListNode]bool)
    current := head

    for current != nil {
        if seen[current] {
            return true
        }

        seen[current] = true
        current = current.Next
    }

    return false
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` expected time.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

The values are never used for cycle detection. Only the node addresses are stored, so repeated values do not cause a false result.

This keeps the nodes already seen in a `HashSet`. Before moving forward, the code checks whether the current node is already in the set. Seeing the same node again means the links brought us back to an earlier place.

For example, if the list is `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 2`, node `2` is found in the set on the second visit. An empty list and a single node without a self-link both return `false`.

```java
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

class Solution {
    public boolean hasCycle(ListNode head) {
        Set<ListNode> seen = new HashSet<>();
        ListNode current = head;

        while (current != null) {
            if (!seen.add(current)) {
                return true;
            }
            current = current.next;
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the stored node references.

The set uses object identity for these node objects, which is what we need. Two different nodes with the same value are not treated as the same node.

A slow pointer moves one node at a time, while a fast pointer moves two nodes. If there is no loop, fast reaches `nullptr`. If a loop exists, the two pointers eventually meet inside it.

The meeting point does not need to be the first node of the loop. We only need to know whether a meeting happens.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool hasCycle(ListNode *head) {
        ListNode* slow = head;
        ListNode* fast = head;

        while (fast != NULL && fast->next != NULL) {
            slow = slow->next;
            fast = fast->next->next;

            if (slow == fast) {
                return true;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The list is only read, so the input links are not changed.

This uses Brent's cycle method instead of moving two pointers on every iteration. One pointer stays at the start of the current search block, and another pointer moves ahead. When the block length reaches a power of two, the first pointer is moved to the current position and the block size doubles.

If the list ends, there is no cycle. If both pointers refer to the same node, a loop was found. It is a little less familiar than Floyd's method, but it still uses constant extra space.

```javascript
/*Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode {
    constructor(val = 0, next = null) {
        this.val = val;
        this.next = next;
    }
}
*/

class Solution {
    hasCycle(head) {
        if (head === null || head.next === null) {
            return false;
        }

        let anchor = head;
        let current = head.next;
        let blockSize = 1;
        let stepsInBlock = 0;

        while (current !== null) {
            if (anchor === current) {
                return true;
            }

            if (stepsInBlock === blockSize) {
                anchor = current;
                blockSize *= 2;
                stepsInBlock = 0;
            }

            current = current.next;
            stepsInBlock++;
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The code handles a one-node self-loop through the normal pointer comparison and does not change any node.

